--- !u!1001 &567978362
PrefabInstance:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  serializedVersion: 2
  m_Modification:
    serializedVersion: 3
    m_TransformParent: {fileID: 1537770733}
    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 726174561797512779, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "Name: Iain Schweblin\r\nAge: 46\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nCreative\r\nSocially
        Capable\r\nStrong will\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear of crowds\r\nFear of loss\r\nFear
        of losing his mind\r\n\nGoal Room: Ritual Room\nFear Room: Ghost Room\n\nCharacter
        Story on Next Page\n\n\n\tIain Schewblin is a middle-aged man. He has a long
        face that leads down to a thin, but well trimmed beard. He wears thick, tortoise
        shell glasses, hooked on large ears. Started balding early so, to save himself
        the trouble, he went bald at the age of 26. He hasn\u2019t grown it out since.
        \r\n\tIain Schweblin is a father of two. When people ask him what he does,
        that\u2019s what he says. If he were to answer the question they were actually
        asking, he\u2019d say he\u2019s  a writer. That is how he makes his money
        and that is what drove him to a small town in Wyoming that would be labeled
        as nowhere, if anyone was there to declare it. Iain certainly never would.
        He loved his life on the empty plains. It was worth the 40-minute drive to
        take his kids to school. It was worth the utility charges. It was all worth
        it to be away from it all.\r\n\tMr. Schweblin was not a shut-in, at least
        not in the traditional sense. He got along with people just fine and had
        no problem making conversation at the bank or the grocery store. He enjoyed
        any chance he had to stretch that muscle. Iain tried to take the kids into
        Cheyenne as often as he could, although they rarely complained. There wasn\u2019t
        much for kids of fifteen and thirteen to do in that town, but they always
        found ways to spend their time. He tried to give the kids space on those
        trips. His childhood was filled with days spent wandering unsupervised, and
        he convinced himself this is what gave him his creative edge. Was he giving
        them some freedom? Was he just a lazy parent? He hadn\u2019t decided. All
        he knew was that he didn\u2019t want to trap them on that vast, empty plain.
        He just couldn\u2019t handle  crowds. \r\n\tThey met in college. She was
        nineteen. He was twenty. She was in his novel writing class. Before that
        meeting, girls weren\u2019t something Iain thought about. At least, that\u2019s
        what he told his friends when they asked about his embarrassing lack of \u201Cgame,\u201D
        as they called it. The truth was he did think about it, probably too much.
        He told himself that eventually he\u2019d have the guts to walk up to someone
        like her and win her over with his quick wit. But it was so much easier to
        live in his head where the risk wasn\u2019t real.\r\n\tShe wrote her name
        on a piece of paper, dropped it on his desk, and walked out of the class.
        Grace Adrian. On the other side, she\u2019d written her number, looping the
        middle of her threes.They had talked maybe two times during class projects.
        She laughed at his jokes, but he didn\u2019t think much of it. But suddenly
        all he did was think about it. He spent the day staring at that piece of
        paper, wondering what to do, what to say.  After making himself  nearly crazy,
        he picked up the phone and dialed. \r\n\tTheirs was a story like that of
        many college sweethearts. She made his writing better, letting him know when
        the pieces didn\u2019t fit together or when his drive to sound smart got
        in the way of his better judgment. They got married a few years after college,and
        the kids came along after that.Grace made her living doing literary criticism,
        and Iain\u2019s books became a hit. He owed it all to her. Without her, the
        books would have been a mess of incoherent ramblings.Grace made everything
        make sense.\r\n\tTwelve years after they got married, Iain took Grace away
        for their anniversary. She wanted to see shows on Broadway. During their
        second day in New York, Iain wanted to see Times Square. Even after Grace
        assured him he wasn\u2019t missing out on anything, they went. The crowd
        was huge during peak tourist season. They waded into the sea of people, but,
        eventually, Iain lost track of her. He looked everywhere, standing on his
        toes to scan the crowd. I When a hole opened in the crowd, he spotted her
        lying on the pavement. \r\n\tFive years later, Iain is driving the kids to
        visit their mom, who now lies in a cemetery in Denver. He leaves the car
        running and asks the kids to stay put for a little while. He walks up the
        narrow path leading to the cemetery.  He\u2019d walked this  path many times,
        both in and out of his head. He kneels, closes his eyes and talks to her
        the way he\u2019d done  the day she\u2019d dropped that note on his desk.
        \r\n\tOpening his eyes, he looks up to her gravestone. Grace Adrian. Loving
        Wife and Mother. He immediately spots the change \u2014 a new carving in
        the stone. The last thing he remembers before waking up is reading the words:
        REDACTED.\n"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
