    - target: {fileID: 5405805202223081092, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "Name: Mackenzie Joyce\r\nAge: 21\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nCautious\r\nConfidence\r\nHard
        Working\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear of falling behind\r\nFear of elderly\r\nFear
        of inadequacy\r\n\nGoal Room: Hidden Treasure Room\nFear Room: Ghost Room\n\n\nCharacter
        Story on Next Page\n\n\n\tMackenzie Joyce is a waitress at a Chinese buffet.
        Mackenzie wears the same thing everyday, practically: jeans and her work
        shirt. Her legs peaking out the bottom of the overworn denim. She would often
        put her strawberry blonde hair up before going in to work. Her work days
        include refilling the drinks of the elderly crowd that rolls in for dinner
        everyday at four. She takes classes at the local community college on the
        weekends, expecting to graduate with a degree in business at the end of the
        year. \r\n\tShe still lives with her parents, a fact she doesn\u2019t mention
        whenever making small talk. She justifies it by saying she is helping take
        care of her little sister, Mia. If you ask Mia, the best thing about having
        her sister at home is that she leaves weed in the top drawer of the dresser
        and never notices when the supply goes down. Mackenzie pays her share in
        rent and utilities. Part of her knows that she would be able to live on her
        own, but another part wonders if she could. For the time being, her bills
        are paid by the retirement funds of the golden girls that need drink refills
        every Friday \u201Cnight.\u201D \r\n\tThe calls started on her opening shift
        while she was sweeping the main room. She pulled her phone out of her apron.
        The call showed no name, not even a \u201Cno caller ID\u201D or an \u201Cunknown,\u201Djust
        a blank space where the number should be. She declined the call. The rest
        of the day went as planned. The regulars came in at their regular time. There
        was rarely any new customers. If there was, they had wandered in after driving
        through town and giving up on finding something better. Two hours before
        closing a new customer came in and sat down. \r\n\tHe was thin and tall.
        He had to almost shrink himself down to fit in the chair. He looked old,
        seventies maybe. His eyes were dark and his hair was sparse and gray. His
        forgettable face had eyes that stared miles past anything in the restaurant.
        He wore a thick coat and dark jeans that ran down into black boots. He stared
        at the table. Mackenzie felt hesitant about going over to him. Most people
        don\u2019t even understand the point of a waitress at a buffet, and this
        man was anything but inviting. \r\nAs time passed, she decided she had to
        greet him. She walked over to his table and set down a glass of water. \u201CWelcome
        in! What can I get you to drink?\u201D He looked up just enough so that she
        knew he heard him. He waved his hand and pointed to the water. One could
        nearly see the bones in his fingers through his thin skin. Mackenzie paused
        for a moment. \u201CWell, let me know if you need anything.\u201D\r\n\tHe
        spent the next hour sipping water, never getting up to get food. If her manager
        was there, he would have probably told her to kick the man out, but it was
        slow tonight. Soon, though, it became time to close and Mackenzie walked
        over one more time. \u201CSorry, sir. We are closing now. Is there anything
        I can get you on your way out?\u201D The man shook his head and began to
        stand up. He turned toward her, seeing her for the first time. \r\n\t\u201CAnswer
        your phone.\u201D\r\n\tSince that day, she has been getting calls more frequently.
        The first few days she got maybe one a day, then they grew. Nearly a week
        after the man wandered in, she was getting nearly seven calls a day. She
        had reached out to her phone company to see if she could block the number,
        but they had no record of the calls. She thought about going to the police
        but had no way to prove it was happening. Finally, Mackenzie decided that
        she could work without a phone and left it on her bedside table.\r\n\tThat
        day as she opened the door to work, the phone to the restaurant rang, an
        old desk phone with a blue screen. She stared at it as it droned on, seemingly
        forever.  She watched the phone light up and her gaze sank into the blue
        void on the phone\u2019s monitor, not listing any name, number, or location.
        It rang until  her manager came over and picked it up. He nodded his head
        to the voice on the other end of the line. He handed the phone to Mackenzie
        saying, \u201CIt\u2019s for you.\u201D \r\nShe wanted to run away. She wanted
        to tell him to hang up the phone and rip it  from the wall, but she couldn\u2019t.
        Her arm, seemingly against her control, reached for the phone. Shaking, she
        brought it to her ear. The last thing she remembered was hearing the words:
        REDACTED.\n"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5565860993906623674, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_IsActive
      value: 0
