    - target: {fileID: 7686131481758405253, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "Name: Harold Auden\r\nAge: 36\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nIntelligence\r\nSkepticism\r\nCaution\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear
        of not being respected\r\nFear of disease (Hypochondriac)\r\nPhysically weak\r\nPhotosensitive\r\n\nGoal
        Room: Study\nFear Room: Dungeon\n\nCharacter Story on Next Page\n\n\tHarold
        Auden is a Professor of Gothic Literature at Miskatonic University. A man
        in his mid-thirties with the face of a pubescent teenager, he shows up to
        his classes in a three-piece suit, no matter the weather. Wearing his father\u2019s
        shoes, he enters the classroom freshly polished every morning. He wears thick
        black glasses that fog up in the cold winter months and carries his books
        in a leather satchel that shows its wear from years of use. A tall, slender
        man, his arms and legs look as if they could snap off from a strong breeze.
        \r\nWaking up to his phone alarm, Harold turns his lamp on, striking his
        eyes. Dr. Auden starts his days with a bowl of oatmeal with brown sugar and
        three blueberries. After which, he drives into work. Arriving at approximately
        seven thirty every morning,  he walks into a lecture hall older than him.
        The tube lights buzz as he flicks the stiff switch, turning his head down
        and quickly sanitizing his hands. He spends an hour reading in front of the
        empty auditorium before preparing for the day\u2019s classes. The classes
        are mainly attended by upperclassmen. Many worry that the decades to come
        could turn them into a version of Professor Auden.  Harold feels the collective
        pity. After the final lecture , he climbs back into the car and heads straight
        home, reading more before shutting the lights off.  \r\n\tHis love of the
        Gothic comes from when he was a child. Harold spent much of his early childhood
        bedridden with an immune deficiency. It left him watching the neighborhood
        kids out a windowpane from the second story of suburban row house. When he
        did get the chance to leave the house, there was always something to be wary
        of. \u201CDon\u2019t touch that.\u201D \u201CDon\u2019t go out in that.\u201D
        \u201CDon\u2019t stand so close to that.\u201D The words of his mother  echoed
        in his ears. Stuck in his room, Harold escaped into books. His librarian
        mother brought home whatever Harold asked, and often there was a recurring
        cast. Lovecraft, Jackson, King. He read as much horror as he could get, naturally
        drawn to the abject and the unspoken. \r\n\tAs Harold grew into a teen and
        his condition improved, he couldn\u2019t shake the isolation. It was familiar
        to him. To school and back, everyday. When it was time to go to college,
        it was obvious what he wanted to do and with a degree like his, there weren\u2019t
        options with anything short of a PhD. So that\u2019s what he did. But after
        only a year or two struggling to become a writer and critic, Harold boomeranged
        right back to Miskatonic University where he used his limited success to
        get a full-time professorship teaching Gothic Literature.  \r\n\tStumbling
        upon a dusty book during his weekend retreats to the college archives, he
        pulled the tome bound with a smooth, thick material off the shelf to investigate.
        The rubber-like material was fraying at the edges but the pages were as white
        as if it had just been printed. The last thing Dr. Auden remembered before
        waking up here was reading the first few words of the book: REDACTED"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 7686131481758405253, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_pageToDisplay
      value: 1
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 8436359322733846254, guid: bb1074ffb16792e4a9319b6d2e820294, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "Name: Evelyn Ventress\r\nAge: 43\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nIntelligence\r\nRational\r\nPhysical
        Strength\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear of being alone forever\r\nFear of being
        ostracized\r\nFear of the unknown\r\n\nGoal Room: Armory\nFear Room: Dungeon\n\nCharacter
        Story on Next Page\n\n\n\tEvelyn Ventress is a psychologist with the Alchemilla
        Clinic. Her features are sharp. The eyes are seemingly permanently wide;
        so brown that it barely distinguishes itself from the black of her pupil.
        When Evelyn speaks to someone, she rarely breaks eye contact, only ever to
        study the other nuances of a face. Her nose is small and the bridge is constantly
        wrinkled as she flips through the pages of a book or sorts through the files
        on her desk. Her long flat lips are pale. She may wear makeup but it\u2019s
        hard for anyone to tell for sure. Her clothes are plain and she doesn\u2019t
        wear rings or jewelry of any kind. She is deceptively muscular. Her lab coat
        conceals the definition in her arms. The people she works with never see
        her without it. \r\n\tShe starts every morning with a workout after turning
        the extra bedroom into a personal gym. She listens to the news or a relevant
        lecture while doing cardio, only pausing to check metrics from the smart
        watch on her wrist. Her breakfast varies but is always balanced. She showers
        quickly, changes, and heads off to work in an electric compact car. She doesn\u2019t
        work with patients anymore. Evelyn put in her time in that department and
        allows that work to be done by her graduate students. All of her efforts
        are put towards the research now. Interpreting and sorting the data that
        comes from her varies studies. The walls of her office are covered in various
        accolades and recognitions. They wouldn\u2019t be on display if they didn\u2019t
        come framed. When five pm comes, she gathers her things and tries to participate
        in friendly conversation with her team. It feels forced for everyone involved.
        \r\n\tShe drives home to get ready. She owns only two things that would be
        seen as passable for a date. Looking at her closet, she criticizes herself
        for her apathy toward it all. The next hour is spent in front of the mirror,
        checking every part of herself to a metric she\u2019s not sure she understands.
        Looking down at the dainty rose gold watch, light on her wrist compared to
        the brick she spends her days with, she knows she has to leave. Evelyn takes
        one more look at herself in the mirror, takes a deep breath, and smiles to
        herself as genuinely as she can muster before starting to leave. She stops
        on her way out, grabbing a small pocket knife on a side table in the vestibule.
        \r\n\tThis was her fifth first date in three months. She had begun to get
        tired of the same small talk. This night something was different. Gene wore
        an expertly tailored suit and he never seemed to stop smiling. She found
        it easy to talk to him. He picked up a lot of the slack, talking when she
        didn\u2019t want to. His eyes would dart fast every which way when he talked
        eventually landing back on hers when he got to the punchline or resolved
        a story. She didn\u2019t think he was particularly funny but she was able
        to fake her way through it convincingly, which was enough for her. He said
        her name, \u201CEvelyn\u201D, with a knowingness that was immediately attention
        grabbing. She couldn\u2019t figure out if it was attractive or creepy.  He
        kept fiddling with his sleeves the entire night, itching at the place where
        his shirt met his wrist. After what she thought was an appropriate amount
        of time, she made some excuse to get home early, making an effort to get
        his number. \r\n\tRight before they were about to go separate ways in the
        parking lot. Gene stopped her. He pulled out an envelope from his back pocket
        and gave it to her. \u201CThis might interest you,\u201D he said. Before
        Evelyn could ask any questions, he walked away. She never saw him drive away.
        He just walked into the blackness of the night. She took the letter home
        and stared at it on the kitchen table. She felt a weird aura coming from
        it. After staring at it for what seemed like an eternity, she pushed aside
        her hesitation as irrational and open the paper. The last thing Evelyn remembers
        before waking up is reading the words: REDACTED"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
